decided  he felt he wasn't quite fluent enough to  be sent to a francophone country so he  was sent to an anglophone country  as an aid officer a fairly senior guy i  presume so a full year salary full-time  study didn't learn the lang french in a  bilingual country like canada didn't  learn it well enough to go and work in a  francophone country um  so you know it all depends on the  individual  it it again relates back to this um  you know i should say i i mean i i'm  sure that at these schools they impose  discipline on you so if you're not  disciplined you need to go to a school  i'm sure they do a lot of drilling  but maybe you know i don't want to sit  in the class and be drilled  uh it might be good but then the problem  with all of those schools is that it  takes away  your own initiative why why are we  learning the language of course if  you're  paid by the army and they say you shall  go and learn a language then you do  whatever they tell you to do  that's understandable  but  if i look at my check for example i'm  sitting there today  i'm reading on my ipad i finished  reading uh an article about  the austro-hungarian empire and learning  a lot of stuff about czech history and  different personalities uh in that  period then i read  an interview  from  the czech radio with someone who was  talking about  who heads up the  institute for the study of totalitarian  regimes in the czech republic and talked  about a number of issues  and i'm reading it and i'm understanding  it and i'm happy i still can't speak  very well i can't speak i haven't spoken  much  but i'm happy so my goals are satisfied  i'm not learning  for someone else i'm learning for me  you know it gets back to this whole  issue of when you're in a classroom and  you read something they ask you to prove  that you understood it  and as ruben alves and anyone that's  into portuguese i recommend that you  google for ruben alves r-u-b-e-m new  word a-l-v-e-s  and find his texts and buy his audio  books from whoever has them on sale it's  it'll really you know propel your  portuguese forward brazilian portuguese  of course  and he's full of wisdom and and a very  good attitude on education but he's as  he says nothing destroys the pleasure of  reading as much as having to analyze  answer questions about the text it's my  text i read it i'll form my  understanding of it i don't need to  answer to you and that's how i feel  other people may like to do that so  that's fine and dandy too it's a bit  like this issue of um  pronunciation and again i have someone  sent me a a video response to my video  where i said it's futile to think that  you're gonna sound like a native uh it's  it's fine to to use uh the native  and that uh you know uh  variant of the language that you find  most attractive or most useful or most  prestigious or whatever and use that as  your model and to try to imitate it is  fine but don't think you're going to  sound like a native  it's highly unlikely  and don't worry about it  and i also made the point that in my  experience the people i meet who speak  english well  uh  and who have an accent don't bother me  in the slightest people who have little  accent but misuse the language uh are a  much much bigger problem but the point  again there is  it's up to you if i'm learning the  language for myself  i don't care my goal is to communicate  to understand people understand me  uh  you know i community i can read i can  connect with the culture i'm happy  so i think it's very important that  everyone  identify what their goal is what is it  they want to do with this language if i  live in vancouver i'm not going to spend  a lot of time  talking to czech people  i may meet some people  i'm not going to make all my friends  check just so that i can speak czech i  have my friends i have a lot of  activities with people some of whom  speak foreign languages i don't choose  my friends based on whether they're  useful to me  in in the language that i'm learning  but  i can watch check movies i can read  check books i can go visit prague i can  get or other places in the czech  republic in fact i'm kind of thinking  now that i'd like to do the other  languages that were in the  austro-hungarian empire or the austrian  empire you know uh maybe learn up some  hungarian and  romanian and uh  polish and croatian and these were all  languages that were  in slovakia  but i guess it's pretty close to czech  that were part of that austro-hungarian  empire italian  uh but i may go back to korean first  because i have why because i play golf  with mr choi who's a very nice guy who  stumbles a bit in his english and would  probably humor me if i spoke to him in  korean so that's a motivation so  it all goes down to what and there my  motivation would be to talk to mr choi  so it all depends on what our motivation  is and we so that we learn languages for  ourselves  and our success depends on ourselves i  know michelle thomas in his tape says  that there's no bad student there's only  a bad teacher i don't believe that i  just don't believe that  other than  it may be that a very good teacher can  stimulate a learner can turn the learner  on to becoming motivated but the the  motivated learner learns and the  unmotivated learner  struggles and i think that's regardless  of the school you go to the teacher or  anything else so what really matters in  language learning is you so i think  people should really sit down and say  why am i learning this language what do  i want to achieve in this language and  then target and also  what do i like doing in this language  there's no point in subjecting yourself  to a method of learning that you don't  like  so find  a method of learning that suits your  tastes like i firmly believe that we  learn the same way that our brains  essentially learn the same way but we do  have different tastes and some people  like to read grammar explanations others  don't  some people only want to watch tv i  personally don't i prefer to listen and  read because i find it more dense in  terms of words and it's  for me it gets my imagi  imagination thinking about you know what  is the meaning in all this if it's  i do watch movies but  it's more as a reward or entertainment  not as a main  uh language learning tool but that's me  and so other people who like to watch tv  that's what they should do so i think  it's very important that what really  matters in language learning is you the  learner why am i learning this language  what do i like doing in the language  uh you know am i motivated how do i make  myself more motivated and when it comes  to being motivated  you know it's important to stay  motivated and therefore it's important  to  uh be satisfied with what you have  achieved like i'm reading check i'm  happy if i speak to yarda i stumble and  can hardly make myself understood i  don't let that ma that discourage me  it's very important once you have  achieved something to continue to enjoy  that and to give yourself credit for  what you have achieved rather than  worrying about what you have yet to  achieve the fact that there are things  that you can still strive for is a good  thing but when you fail or in your own  mind you failed or you forgot a word or  you didn't communicate as well as you  would have liked not a problem uh the  more you're sort of  unhappy about what you are unable to do  in the language the less well you will  learn because if you feel good about  your language studies  if you feel confident if you feel that  you're achieving something  um  you know we know  even in terms of that they measure what  goes on in the brain the brain's happy  if the brain achieves  a little better than what expected to  what it expected to achieve  uh it is motivated to learn more so  don't put obstacles in front of yourself  by uh  being frustrated that you don't speak  like a native or that you forget words  or that you continue to have trouble  with certain structures or certain  passages remain unclear to you that  doesn't matter focus  rather on what you have achieved it'll  make you happier and and you will learn  better so here again in terms of  attitude in terms of choosing what to  study in terms of motivating yourself  the key what matters most in language  learning is you the learner  thank you for listening bye for now

